{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","language":"python","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.11"}},"nbformat_minor":5,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"id":"b9285194-3cdb-4e49-88b2-ae40c840bd0a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Week 8 — Gradient Boosting II: LightGBM + SHAP + Model Stacking\n**Date:** July 28, 2026\n**Week:** 8 of 10 — follows Week 7 (XGBoost, closed out; SHAP explicitly deferred to this week)\n\nXGBoost edged out RF as your best single model this week just closed, but by a thin margin, and RF's two weak spots (`class_target_2`, `class_target_3_corrected` LONG) got closed rather than blown open — worth keeping in mind before this week's tools invite overconfidence. This week has four real jobs, in order: (1) finally compile the RF-vs-XGBoost win/loss tally Week 7 never assembled, so \"current best\" means something concrete; (2) add LightGBM as a third tree ensemble and run SHAP across all three models together — the interpretability pass Week 7 deliberately postponed rather than redoing later; (3) build a stacking ensemble to see whether combining model families beats any single one; (4) do a genuine mid-point review of every model trained since Week 1, plus a first look at regime-dependence (trending vs ranging markets). None of this is a free pass to treat this week's numbers as more solid than last week's — SHAP agreement, stacking gains, and regime splits are all new places for optimistic bias to hide if you don't check for it the same way you checked Week 4's `class_target_3`.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"0dab891a-e8c8-442b-a404-c598b1b6d599","cell_type":"markdown","source":"---","metadata":{}},{"id":"eacba469-aeca-4eef-8912-e298714a16f3","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## PART A — Setup (carried forward from Week 7, complete)","metadata":{}},{"id":"12bb253b-c36e-449d-89ec-6a08f077a28d","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### A.0 — Carrying Week 7's lessons forward, explicitly\n\nTwo of these bit you again *inside