ount":null},{"id":"a5c7b6c6-602d-45e6-9702-ebdb44159a30","cell_type":"markdown","source":"---","metadata":{}},{"id":"d48e7971-b3d1-40a1-a23c-79fe32104fa6","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## PART B — LightGBM Theory + Implementation (Monday)","metadata":{}},{"id":"01009193-ca07-4088-bfcd-b0b5ddc38a98","cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Concept:** LightGBM grows trees **leaf-wise** (best-loss-reduction leaf split next) rather than **level-wise** (every leaf at a depth splits before moving deeper, XGBoost's default). Leaf-wise can reach a lower loss with fewer splits, but it's easier to overfit on a small/noisy leaf if `num_leaves`/`min_data_in_leaf` aren't controlled — think about which of your Week 6/7 depth findings (shallow depth generally best, `min_samples_leaf` interacting with depth) this predicts for LightGBM's own controls.\n\n**Histogram binning:** continuous features are pre-bucketed into a fixed number of histogram bins before split-finding, which is why LightGBM is typically faster on large tabular datasets than XGBoost's exact or approximate split search.","metadata":{}},{"id":"5a5ab449-ac42-4dec-8052-28c6d087217f","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Deliverable — Leaf-wise vs level-wise, in your own words (fill in)","metadata":{}},{"id":"983ad140-e7d6-47b8-bb7b-c4db343c2296","cell_type":"markdown","source":"*(your notes here — specifically: given Week 6/7's finding that shallow trees win on this dataset, what does that predict about how tightly you'll need to constrain `num_leaves` here?)*","metadata":{}},{"id":"25f61c01-836f-4c11-a237-2ce9d0fee110","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Train LGBMClassifier / LGBMRegressor with early stopping","metadata":{}},{"id":"4c5a3a34-0bab-4c19-9879-3f4d89f1e32b","cell_type":"code","source":"# ============================================================\n# TODO: Train LGBMClassifier with early stopping, per classification target\n# ============================================================\n# TODO: mirror Week 